Networks compiled into crate shotlab by burn-onnx (build.rs), under permissive licences whose texts are next to this
file (the sky network made from its checkpoint by efficientvit.py and onnxslim, below). Each checked against
onnxruntime on real input (run when X5_ORT names a Python with onnxruntime): src/clone.rs `vs_onnxruntime` (a 192² crop
of a woman in v627 frame 300), largest difference of the person probability 2.2e-6; src/sky.rs `vs_onnxruntime`
(below).

human_segmentation_pphumanseg_2023mar.onnx
  What: PP-HumanSeg v1 "humanseg_mobile" (PaddleSeg contrib/HumanSeg): FCN on HRNet-W18-Small v1, person
        segmentation, 1x3x192x192 RGB as (c - 0.5) / 0.5 -> 1x2x192x192 softmax (background, person). Converted to
        ONNX by OpenCV (PaddlePaddle's humanseg_hrnet18_small_v1 inference model through paddle2onnx, opset 11;
        samples/dnn/dnn_model_runner/dnn_conversion/paddlepaddle in opencv 4.x) and published in the OpenCV model zoo.
  Source: https://github.com/opencv/opencv_zoo/tree/main/models/human_segmentation_pphumanseg
          (downloaded from the zoo's mirror https://huggingface.co/opencv/human_segmentation_pphumanseg/resolve/main/human_segmentation_pphumanseg_2023mar.onnx;
          the mirror's LFS oid is the sha256 below)
  Licence: Apache-2.0 ("All files in this directory are licensed under Apache 2.0 License", the directory's README
           and LICENSE; Copyright (c) 2021 PaddlePaddle Authors) -> LICENSE-pphumanseg.
  Training data: "Baidu self-built dataset", no published terms (PaddleHub's humanseg_mobile, HRNet_w18_small_v1 like
           this one: https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleHub/blob/develop/modules/image/semantic_segmentation/humanseg_mobile/README_en.md).
           PaddleSeg's HumanSeg README (release/2.1) says the released models were trained on "large-scale portrait
           data" without naming it, and uses a subset of Supervisely Persons for its fine-tuning example; the weights
           are published under Apache-2.0 by their authors.
  sha256: 552d8a984054e59b5d773d24b9b12022b22046ceb2bbc4c9aaeaceb36a9ddf24
  Build fix: burn-onnx 0.22 resolves 6 of the network's 20 stride-2 3x3 convolutions (SAME_UPPER on even inputs: pad
  0 before, 1 after) as padding 1 on both sides; build.rs rewrites them in the generated code and asserts the counts.
  Considered, on perspective crops of people in v627 (night) and v606 (day), against this network at 192²:
  - MediaPipe selfie segmentation, ONNX from https://huggingface.co/onnx-community/mediapipe_selfie_segmentation
    (Apache-2.0, 0.45 MB, 256² input, sha256 3241ac4ad8aa35bdaf33946776db29f7c283a413aa0b0dacb9483594b4531aad):
    made for a face close to the camera; it lost whole people at full-body size (a woman in a brown coat, a man at
    the frame's edge).
  - MODNet portrait matting, ONNX from https://huggingface.co/Xenova/modnet (Apache-2.0, 24.7 MB, 512² input, sha256
    07c308cf0fc7e6e8b2065a12ed7fc07e1de8febb7dc7839d7b7f15dd66584df9): mattes for portraits; on small full-body
    people it left the torso out or covered only fragments.
  - This network with its input scaled to 384² (Resize targets doubled): legs went missing; 192² is kept.
  Not used: Robust Video Matting (GPL-3.0).

efficientvit_seg_b1_ade20k_6x384.onnx
  What: EfficientViT-seg B1 (Cai et al., "EfficientViT: Lightweight Multi-Scale Attention for High-Resolution Dense
        Prediction", ICCV 2023, https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14756), 4.8 M parameters, trained on ADE20K by its authors
        (42.8 mIoU at 512², their README): 6x3x384x384 RGB normalised by ImageNet's mean and deviation -> 6x150x48x48
        class logits (stride 8, the head upsamples bicubically); src/seg.rs takes the softmax's class 2, sky. Six tiles
        of 120° at pitch 35° around the world's up a run (src/sky.rs).
  Source: the authors' checkpoint,
          https://huggingface.co/han-cai/efficientvit-seg/resolve/main/efficientvit_seg_b1_ade20k.pt (sha256
          59cca6a7ff4d1a6494b7f12072c6a68fee40ef401e971f26af01a2e0f3c8f5e7), linked from the
          applications/efficientvit_seg README of https://github.com/mit-han-lab/efficientvit. Exported by
          efficientvit.py (`python efficientvit.py efficientvit_seg_b1_ade20k.pt raw.onnx`, torch 2.14 and onnx 1.23:
          the network written out after the repository's efficientvit/models, the checkpoint loaded weights-only and
          strictly, so none of the repository's code runs), then given static shapes by onnxslim 0.1.99 (`onnxslim
          raw.onnx efficientvit_seg_b1_ade20k_6x384.onnx`: shape arithmetic folded). onnxruntime against PyTorch on the
          same random input: logits within 3.3e-4.
  Licence: Apache-2.0: the model card's "license: apache-2.0" (huggingface.co/han-cai/efficientvit-seg) and the
           repository's LICENSE, "Copyright [2023] [Han Cai]" -> LICENSE-efficientvit. No further terms on the weights
           in the card or the README.
  Training data: ADE20K, whose images MIT CSAIL does not own and lets researchers use "only for non-commercial research
           and educational purposes", its annotations BSD-3-Clause (https://ade20k.csail.mit.edu/terms, read through the
           Wayback Machine: the site timed out); the backbone "initialized with weights pretrained on ImageNet" (the
           paper's experimental setup), ImageNet "only for non-commercial research and educational purposes"
           (https://image-net.org/accessagreement.php).
  sha256: 3867a774e932636a91c14b7d287c2d4829dae8e6990c03792264de1c93e954b9
  Checked: src/sky.rs `vs_onnxruntime` (the six tiles of v606 frame 300, the gate under a blue sky): largest
           difference of the sky probability 4.1e-6 (mean 1.2e-7), none on the other side of ½. The authors' code was
           not run; efficientvit.py loads every tensor of the checkpoint by name and shape, and its masks agree with
           SegFormer-B0's by day (below).
  Considered (licences read 2026-11-21; masks by onnxruntime on the CPU over 15 frames of the samples and the rides at
  3840, horizon lock, six 384² tiles of 120° at pitch 35°, against SegFormer-B0's as below):
  - SegFormer-B0 fine-tuned on ADE20K (nvidia/segformer-b0-finetuned-ade-512-512, NVIDIA Source Code License for
    SegFormer): dropped. Its "non-commercially" means "for research or evaluation purposes only" (the licence's
    section 3.3), too narrow for this project. Its masks are the reference below; the pass took 56 ms a frame on the
    Radeon 8060S with it (decode head rewritten), 22 ms with B1 (3840 wide).
  - EfficientViT-seg B2 on ADE20K (same source and licence, 15 M parameters, 58 MB): agrees with B1 by day; at night
    and through small openings it finds more sky (714 at night 0.30 of the upper hemisphere against B1's 0.12 and
    SegFormer's 0.19; the far end of a bridge 0.044 against 0.003 and 0.023), at three times B1's size and 51 ms a
    frame on the Radeon.
  - EfficientViT-seg B0 and B1 on Cityscapes (same source and licence; Cityscapes' terms not read, as neither was
    chosen): no sky at night (v627 0.007 and 0.50 against 0.73), and they lost much of the rides' day skies.
  - B1 with tiles of 512²: no better at night (714 0.09).
  - Not evaluated: TopFormer, SegNeXt (both Apache-2.0, built on mmsegmentation and mmcv, whose code would have to
    run to export them; TopFormer's ADE20K weights on Baidu and Google Drive only), PP-MobileSeg / PP-LiteSeg
    (PaddleSeg, Apache-2.0; converting them needs PaddlePaddle and paddle2onnx), the CSAIL ADE20K baselines
    (semantic-segmentation-pytorch, BSD-3-Clause; their weights at sceneparsing.csail.mit.edu, which timed out),
    xiongzhu666's U2-Net sky model (MIT code; its 167 MB ONNX on Baidu only, the small one in ncnn's format, training
    data unnamed).
